WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.142 --> 00:00:01.861
(dynamic music)

00:00:01.861 --> 00:00:04.684
- There are very few
prominent black cosplayers.

00:00:04.684 --> 00:00:05.517
I definitely feel like

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:07.054
a minority within a minority sometimes.

00:00:07.054 --> 00:00:10.149
(dynamic music)

00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:12.840
Cosplay literally stands for costume play,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.652
so it's individuals who
dress up in costumes.

00:00:15.652 --> 00:00:17.326
You get to embody the characters.

00:00:17.326 --> 00:00:20.234
It's almost like making an
action figure of yourself.

00:00:20.234 --> 00:00:21.469
My name is Jourdan Barnett.

00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:22.814
By day, I'm a web content manager,

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:25.064
but by night, I'm Deadshot.

00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:28.195
For me, cosplay is a creative outlet.

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:29.832
I'm not an artist, I can't draw,

00:00:29.832 --> 00:00:31.992
but I love to build, I love to craft,

00:00:31.992 --> 00:00:33.422
do things with my hands.

00:00:33.422 --> 00:00:35.281
Each costume is an art piece.

00:00:35.281 --> 00:00:37.641
Cosplayers know me as Blerd Vision.

00:00:37.641 --> 00:00:40.352
Blerd literally stands for black nerd.

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:41.704
Within the cosplay community in general,

00:00:41.704 --> 00:00:42.788
I feel accepted,

00:00:42.788 --> 00:00:44.843
but the majority of the
characters I cosplay

00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:46.670
were originally Caucasian characters.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.069
I definitely feel the racial tension.

00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:50.375
Even with my Flash cosplay,

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:53.036
people thought at first
I was a Reverse-Flash.

00:00:53.036 --> 00:00:55.734
I'm clearly not like that
character you think I am,

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:57.115
so why assume I’m the villain?

00:00:57.115 --> 00:00:59.558
It's really disconcerting
when you love a character,

00:00:59.558 --> 00:01:01.801
but because you don't actually
look like the character,

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.823
people will kind of judge you for that.

00:01:03.823 --> 00:01:05.555
For me and the people
that I associate with,

00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:07.615
it's more about what you love.

00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:09.039
And if you love the character,

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:10.706
play that character.

00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:12.686
Comic books in general

00:01:12.686 --> 00:01:15.825
are starting to push
characters in the direction

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:17.865
of being more diverse.

00:01:17.865 --> 00:01:21.300
Whether it be gay, straight, black, white,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.726
whatever it is,

00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:25.234
they're changing the old guard of heroes

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.577
to a more modern version of them.

00:01:27.577 --> 00:01:28.579
So it should be a reflection of, like,

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:31.006
what our society looks like today.

00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:32.033
Hopefully in the future,

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:34.499
that actually changes people's mentalities

00:01:34.499 --> 00:01:37.174
toward these characters
and what you can be.

00:01:37.174 --> 00:01:38.969
It's a powerful thing when
you can look at a character

00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:40.877
and go that's me.

00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:44.156
It gives you the idea that
you actually do anything,

00:01:44.156 --> 00:01:45.334
if you actually can identify with someone

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:47.152
that can do anything.

00:01:47.152 --> 00:01:50.902
(contemporary ambient music)

